Alice and Bob are playing a game with a fair six-sided die and a function \(f(x)\) defined as follows: 
\[ f(x) = \left\{ \begin{array}{ll}
x^2 & \text{if } x \text{ is even} \\
x^3 & \text{if } x \text{ is odd}
\end{array} \right. \]
They take turns rolling the die, with Alice going first. After each roll, the value of \(f(x)\) for the rolled number \(x\) is added to a running total. The game ends when the total exceeds 100. What is the probability that Alice's final roll causes the game to end?

All real numbers and all negative integers are colored either red or blue, such that the following two conditions are satisfied:

(i) At least one of the numbers \(0\) and \(1\) is colored blue.

(ii) If \(m\) and \(n\) are of the same color and \(|m - n| = 5\), then \(m + n\) is red.

Q: The color of the number \(2012\) is:
(A) Red if and only if so is \(-1\);
(B) Red if and only if so is \(1\);
(C) Blue if and only if so is \(-5\);
(D) Blue if and only if so is \(1\).

Define a sequence \( (a_n) \) by \( a_1 = 1 \) and for \( n \geq 1 \), \[ a_{n+1} = \begin{cases} 
      a_n + \frac{1}{a_n} & \text{if } a_n \text{ is an integer}, \\
      a_n - \frac{1}{a_n} & \text{if } a_n \text{ is not an integer}.
   \end{cases}
\]
Determine the smallest positive integer \( k \) such that \( a_k \) is an integer and \( k \) is also a perfect square.

Find all triples of real numbers (x, y, z) satisfying the system of equations:
\begin{aligned}
& x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 1,\\
& x^3 + y^3 + z^3 = 1 + xyz.
\end{aligned}

Alice writes down a polynomial with integer coefficients, and Bob guesses a real number (in an effort to compute the value of the polynomial at that point). Alice does not tell Bob what the polynomial is, but she will answer Bob’s questions by saying whether or not the value that the polynomial takes on at Bob’s chosen point is an integer. Which of the following is true?

Choose from:
1. Bob can determine, after a finite number of questions, whether there exists an integer such that the polynomial takes the value at ;
2. Bob can determine, after a finite number of questions, whether there exists an integer such that the polynomial takes the value at but he cannot determine, with a finite number of questions, whether there exists an integer such that the polynomial takes the value at ;
3. Bob cannot determine, with a finite number of questions, whether there exists an integer such that the polynomial takes the value at or whether there exists an integer such that the polynomial takes the value at but he can determine, with a finite number of questions, whether there exists an integer such that the polynomial takes the value at ;
4. Bob cannot determine, with a finite number of questions, whether there exists an integer such that the polynomial takes the value at or whether there exists an integer such that the polynomial takes the value at ;
5. None of these

Consider a sequence of positive integers \((a_n)\) defined by \(a_1 = 1\) and for \(n \geq 1\), \[
a_{n+1} = \begin{cases} 
2a_n & \text{if } a_n \text{ is odd}, \\
a_n / 2 & \text{if } a_n \text{ is even}.
\end{cases}
\]Determine the smallest positive integer \(k\) such that \(a_k = a_{2023}\).

What is the smallest positive integer \( n \) such that both of these conditions are met simultaneously:

(1) \( n \) has at least one prime divisor, and that divisor can be expressed as a sum of cubes of three positive integers;
(2) \( n \) can be expressed as the difference of fourth powers of two distinct integers.

Given a regular hexagon \(ABCDEF\) inscribed in a circle of radius \(1\), find the minimum value of \[
\frac{AC^2 + AD^2 + AE^2 + AF^2}{AB^2 + AC^2 + AD^2 + AE^2 + AF^2}
\] over all possible choices of vertex labels \(A, B, C, D, E, F\).

Alice and Bob play a game on an infinite grid of unit squares. Alice places a rook somewhere on the grid. On each turn, Bob can either "vertically translate" (where the rook cannot pass through already-visited unit cells) or "rotate" the rook by 90 degrees around a cell that the rook has previously visited. Alice wins if and only if Bob cannot move the rook on his turn (including when the rook is unable to initially move; note that Bob does not win by taking the rook's initial position). If we allow Alice to place the rook in any initial position, in how many ways can she place it so that she can always win?

For example, if Alice places the rook at the position $(0, 0)$, then Bob can choose between the three moves shown (in orange, green, and blue below). With any of these moves, Bob can reach a position where the rook is unable to move, but Alice can place the rook anywhere so that she can always win.
