A sequence of positive integers \( a_1, a_2, a_3, \ldots \) is defined recursively by \( a_1 = 2 \) and for \( n \geq 1 \):
\[ a_{n+1} = \begin{cases} 
a_n^2 - 1 & \text{if } a_n \text{ is even} \\
\frac{a_n + 1}{2} & \text{if } a_n \text{ is odd}
\end{cases} \]
Let \( S_k \) denote the sum of the digits of \( a_k \) when written in base 3. Find the smallest positive integer \( k \) such that \( S_k \) is a perfect square.

There are six distinct positive integers \(a, b, c, d, e, f\) with the following properties:
- \(a, b, c\) form an arithmetic progression;
- \(b, c, d\) form a geometric progression;
- \(c, d, e\) form an arithmetic progression;
- \(d, e, f\) form a geometric progression;
- If the number \(a\) is exactly divisible by 166, find the minimum possible value for \(a, b, c, d, e, f\).

Let $P(x)$ be a polynomial with integer coefficients such that $P(0) = 1$ and $P(1) = 3$. Suppose there exist integers $a$ and $b$ with $a \neq b$ such that $P(a) = P(b) = 2$. Define the sequence $\{a_n\}_{n=1}^{\infty}$ by $a_1 = 1$, $a_2 = 3$, and for all $n \geq 2$,
\[a_{n+1} = \begin{cases} 
a_n + P(a_n) & \text{if } a_n \text{ is odd} \\
\frac{a_n}{2} + P\left(\frac{a_n}{2}\right) & \text{if } a_n \text{ is even}
\end{cases}\]

Prove that for some positive integer $k$, $a_k = a_{k+1}$, and determine the largest possible value of $k$.

In the figure below, \(ABCD\) is a square of side length 6. \(E\) is the midpoint of side \(BC\), and \(F\) is the midpoint of side \(CD\). Segments \(AE\) and \(AF\) intersect at points \(G\) and \(H\) inside the square. What is the area of quadrilateral \(AGHF\)?

